1.71"></path></svg></button></div></div></h2><p>Gemma 4 models generate tex=
t autoregressively and produce one token at a time. Roughly the same amount=
 of compute is needed for each token, regardless of how difficult it is to =
predict a given token. As such, this can be an unnecessarily slow process w=
hen the tokens are quite easy to predict.</p><p><span>Imagine that the larg=
er model is generating text and has already created =E2=80=9C</span><em>Act=
ions speak</em><span>=E2=80=9D. For those that recognize the start of this =
sentence, it is a common proverb in the English language and extends to =E2=
=80=9C</span><em>Actions speak </em><span>louder </span><em>than words.</em=
><span>=E2=80=9D. Since it is quite common, smaller models are likely to ge=
nerate the exact same completion (=E2=80=9C</span><em>louder than words</em=
><span>=E2=80=9D) as a larger model would. As such, it would be a waste of =
time and compute for the larger model to predict =E2=80=9C</span><em>louder=
 than words</em><span>=E2=80=9D one token at a time.</span></p><p><span>Wit=
h speculative decoding, we can use a smaller draft model to predict several=
 tokens ahead of time. The draft model will receive the same input =E2=80=
=9C</span><em>Actions speak</em><span>=E2=80=9D and still autoregressively =
predict a number of tokens, let=E2=80=99s say four tokens. The drafted toke=
ns are generated much faster than the larger model would as the draft model=
 is only a fraction of its size.</span></p><div class=3D"captioned-image-co=
